  "worked_solution": "The dot product rule states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=c\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the dot-product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =c\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-i+j+3\\,k)\\cdot (16\\,i-10\\,j+4\\,k) \\\\  & =-16-10+12 \\\\  & =-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
